 ## Background
-The concept of animatable avatars using 3D Gaussian Splatting emerged as an extension of static reconstruction methods to handle dynamic subjects. Early works like GaussianAvatar incorporated optimizable tensors with dynamic appearance networks to capture dynamics [S1]. Human101 further optimized the training speed for high-fidelity dynamic human creation, achieving 100 seconds using a fixed-perspective camera [S1][S2, unofficial]. Subsequent methods like SplattingAvatar and GoMAvatar embedded Gaussians onto canonical human body meshes, determining positions via barycentric coordinates and normal displacements [S1][S2, unofficial]. For head modeling, MonoGaussianAvatar applied canonical space modeling and deformation prediction [S2, unofficial], while GaussianHead introduced tri-plane representations and motion fields for continuous movement simulation [S2, unofficial]. HeadGas utilized latent features weighted by 3DMM expression vectors for real-time animatable heads [S2, unofficial]. Extensions to other body parts include 3DPSHR for real-time hand reconstruction using MANO priors [S2, unofficial] and MANUS for hand-object interaction [S2, unofficial]. GART extended these principles to non-human articulated models like animals [S1][S2, unofficial], and D3GA introduced tetrahedral cages for full-body human modeling [S1].
